{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And you shall put into the breastplate of judgment the Urim and the Tummim</b>. Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] sought to be clever regarding the matter of the Urim and the Tummim, and said (Exodus 28:6) that they are the work of a craftsman in silver and gold, and he enlarged upon their matter, for he thought that they were upon the forms that the astrologers make to know the thought of the one who asks. But he said nothing at all. Rather, they are as Rashi's words (Rashi on Exodus 28:30): the explicitly written Name [of God] was placed between the folds of the breastplate, and therefore it was necessary for it to be doubled (Exodus 28:16). And the proof is that Urim and Tummim are not mentioned at all in the work of the craftsmen, nor were they mentioned to them in the commandment or in the making at all. And in the account of the garments he specified: \"And he made the ephod\" (Exodus 39:2), \"And he made the breastplate\" (ibid. v. 8), but he did not say 'and he made the Urim and the Tummim' — whereas if they had been the work of a skilled craftsman he would have enlarged upon them more than all the rest, and even if he wished to abbreviate concerning them because of their profundity, he would have said: 'And you shall make Urim and Tummim as He showed you on the mountain; pure gold you shall make them,' or refined silver.<br>Furthermore, you will see that Scripture did not use the definite article (ה׳ הידיעה) for any of the vessels that had not already been mentioned; rather it said: \"And they shall make an ark\" (Exodus 25:10), \"And you shall make a table\" (ibid. v. 23), \"And you shall make a lampstand\" (ibid. v. 31), and so with all of them. And concerning the Tabernacle it said: \"And you shall make the Tabernacle\" (ibid. 26:1), with the definite article, because it had already mentioned it: \"And they shall make Me a sanctuary\" (ibid. 25:8). But behold, concerning the Urim and Tummim it said (here): \"And you shall put into the breastplate of judgment the Urim and the Tummim\" — He did not command him concerning their making, and Scripture mentioned them with the definite article, and Scripture mentioned them only with Moses alone, for in the commandment it says: \"And you shall put into the breastplate of judgment,\" and in the execution: \"And he placed into the breastplate the Urim and the Tummim\" (Leviticus 8:8). For they were not the work of a craftsman, and the craftsmen had no part in them, nor did the congregation of Israel have any making or donation in them at all; rather, they are a secret entrusted to Moses from the mouth of the Almighty, and he wrote them in holiness, or they were a work of Heaven. Therefore Scripture mentions them without specification and with the definite article, as in: \"And He stationed east of the Garden of Eden the cherubim\" (Genesis 3:24).<br>Behold, Moses took the writing of the Urim and the Tummim and placed them there in the breastplate of judgment after he clothed Aaron with the ephod and the breastplate, as it says (Leviticus 8:7–8): \"And he placed upon him the ephod and girded him with the band of the ephod, and he put upon him the breastplate, and he placed into the breastplate the Urim and the Tummim\" — for only afterward did he place them between the folds of the breastplate.<br>The matter is this: they were holy Names. Through their power the letters from the stones of the breastplate would light up to the eyes of the priest who asks in their judgment (Numbers 27:21). The illustration: when they asked, \"Who shall go up for us first against the Canaanite to fight?\" (Judges 1:1), the priest would concentrate with the Names that are the Urim, and the letters יהודה would light up before his eyes — יו״ד from לוי, עי״ן from שמעון, למ״ד from לוי, ה״א from אברהם, which Scripture places there according to the opinion of our Sages (Yoma 73b). Or perhaps the ה״א from יהודה lit up at another time before his eyes. Now, when the letters light up to the eyes of the priest, he still does not know their arrangement, for from the letters arranged as 'יהודה יעלה' it would have been possible to form 'הויהד עליה' or 'היעל ידוהה', and many other combinations. But there were other holy Names there called 'Tummim,' through whose power the heart of the priest would be perfected (תמים) in knowing the meaning of the letters that had lit up before his eyes. For when he concentrated on the Names of the Urim and they lit up, he would immediately turn and concentrate on the Names of the Tummim. While the letters were still illuminated before his eyes, the arrangement 'יהודה יעלה' would come into his heart. This is one of the levels of the Holy Spirit — it is below prophecy, and above the bat kol (heavenly echo) that was used in the Second Temple after prophecy had ceased, and after the Urim and Tummim had ceased, as our Sages mentioned (ibid. 21b).<br>It is possible that after Moses placed the holy Names of the Urim and Tummim in the breastplate, they became known by tradition from him to the great sages of Israel, to whom he transmitted them along with the hidden secrets of the Torah. Therefore David had an ephod (1 Samuel 23:6), and it was in the likeness of the ephod of Moses, and it had a breastplate in the likeness of the holy breastplate; but it appears that they were made of linen, as it is said concerning Samuel: \"a youth girded with a linen ephod\" (1 Samuel 2:18), and concerning Nob, the city of the priests (ibid. 22:18): \"eighty-five men bearing a linen ephod.\" And they would clothe the priest who was among the sons of the prophets with it and ask through it, and they would be answered on occasion — as R. Abraham [Ibn Ezra] thought regarding this. As for what he said that had he seen the answer of our Master the Rav [Maimonides] he would not have said thus — we have already seen it and considered it, and we know that R. Abraham [Ibn Ezra] did not intend to address it:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :register {:flags {0 ["God" "the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ר\"א is resolved as Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra), consistent with Ramban's references throughout this passage; the abbreviation תיב\"ע is resolved as תיבת בעל כוכבים ('the astrologers' column/form'), reflecting Ibn Ezra's actual interpretation that Ramban critiques."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:17:40.361995439Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}